  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1217 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nSunday will be similar to Saturday and the threat for locally \nheavy rainfall will focus west of the central mountain chain, \nespecially across the southwest mountains where a Flash Flood \nWatch may be required pending soil conditions from the previous \ntwo day's rounds of rainfall. The latest medium range model \nsolutions are showing less spread going into next week and are \nforecasting a sprawling upper high to dominate most of the \nsouthern US, with NM and the Desert SW on the western periphery of\nthe circulation. The orientation of the ridge axis will favor the\nwestern and northern mountains of NM for daily rounds of \nconvection, but a gradual drying of the atmosphere is forecast \nfrom mid through late next week as the monsoon moisture plume \nshifts west into AZ. This all adds up to continued above average \ntemperatures across eastern and portions of central NM, with a \nmoderate heat risk across the eastern plains. Daily rounds for \nstorms from mid through late week will become less rainfall \nproductive as the stubborn upper high remains centered over the US\nSouthern Plains.",
